    <div class="eyebrow"><span>Independent Macro Weekly</span><span class="lozenge">Five official releases</span></div>
    <h1 id="headline">Five releases.<br><span>Three macro dials.</span></h1>
    <p class="dek">From September job openings to October payrolls, this November 2–6 evidence review connects official U.S. labor, Treasury, trade and productivity reports to potential USD transmission channels—without claiming an observed market move.</p>
    <div class="hero-foot">
      <div><strong>Period covered:</strong> 2–6 November 2026</div>
      <div><strong>Scope:</strong> Weekly reporting period, not first-publication date</div>
      <div><strong>Source basis:</strong> Five dated official releases</div>
    </div>
  </div></section>
  <main id="main" class="container layout">
    <article class="content">
      <div class="mw-ai-disclosure" role="note"><strong>AI-assisted preparation.</strong> AI tools assisted in preparing this educational analysis. The cited U.S. government releases support the specified factual anchors, while the conditional interpretations are original explanations rather than observed market movements. USD Impact is operated by KELA LEADS S.R.L. Any public release requires an accountable editor to approve the final copy, source integrity and disclosures.</div>
      <section aria-labelledby="summary"><div class="section-kicker">01 / Executive summary</div><h2 id="summary">The evidence is dated. The dollar response is not measured.</h2>
        <div class="callout"><p><strong>Reporting cutoff and review date:</strong> This article covers releases dated November 2–6 only. Its November 9/10/12 Treasury auction calendar was announced during that week; as reviewed on November 13, all three announced November 9, 10 and 12 auction dates have passed. <strong>No auction outcome, November 10 CPI result or later market movement is implied or included in these five weekly evidence items.</strong> This five-source review is not a complete closing-market or after-period corrections analysis.</p></div>
        <p>This week brought two U.S. labor releases, a quarterly refunding announcement, September international-trade figures and preliminary third-quarter productivity. Together they provide inputs for assessing employment, financing conditions and efficiency. They are <strong>not</strong> a measurement of the dollar or its reaction to any announcement.</p>
        <p>The releases cover different <strong>reference periods</strong>: September for job openings and trade, the third quarter for productivity and October for payroll employment. Their public release dates fall between November 3 and 6. These two kinds of dates must not be confused.</p>
        <div class="callout"><p><strong>Interpretation rule:</strong> Identify the issuer and reference period first, consider the possible rate and risk channels second, and only describe a realized market response when independently verified, rights-cleared prices support it. No USD Impact Score or live market feed is used here.</p></div>
      </section>
      <section aria-labelledby="timeline"><div class="section-kicker">02 / Verified chronology</div><h2 id="timeline">What the original releases reported</h2>
        <div class="timeline">
          <div class="event" data-claim="jolts-nov03"><time datetime="2026-11-03">Tuesday, November 3 · Labor demand</time><h3>September job openings: approximately 7.5 million</h3><p>BLS reported little change in September job openings at <strong>7.5 million</strong> and in hires at <strong>5.4 million</strong>. Quits increased to <strong>3.4 million</strong>. These are September labor-flow measures published in November, not a same-day payroll estimate.</p><a class="source-link" href="https://www.bls.gov/news.release/archives/jolts_11032026.htm" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">BLS · September Job Openings and Labor Turnover · Nov 3</a></div>
          <div class="event" data-claim="treasury-nov04"><time datetime="2026-11-04">Wednesday, November 4 · Treasury financing</time><h3>Treasury announced $125 billion of refinancing securities</h3><p>The Treasury offered <strong>$125 billion</strong> in three-, ten- and thirty-year securities to refund approximately <strong>$97 billion</strong> of maturing privately held debt, raising about <strong>$28 billion</strong> of new cash. This is an announcement of supply and planned auctions—not a completed auction or an observed yield response.</p><a class="source-link" href="https://home.treasury.gov/news/press-releases/sb0678" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">U.S. Treasury · Quarterly Refunding Statement · Nov 4</a></div>
          <div class="event" data-claim="trade-nov04"><time datetime="2026-11-04">Wednesday, November 4 · External trade</time><h3>September trade deficit: $105.0 billion</h3><p>BEA and the Census Bureau reported a September goods-and-services deficit of <strong>$105.0 billion</strong>, down <strong>$0.7 billion</strong> from revised August. September exports totaled <strong>$321.5 billion</strong> and imports <strong>$426.6 billion</strong>. The statistics describe September trade, not a November currency-price move.</p><a class="source-link" href="https://www.bea.gov/news/2026/us-international-trade-goods-and-services-september-2026" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">BEA/Census · U.S. International Trade, September · Nov 4</a></div>
          <div class="event" data-claim="productivity-nov05"><time datetime="2026-11-05">Thursday, November 5 · Production efficiency</time><h3>Third-quarter productivity increased 3.6% annualized</h3><p>Preliminary BLS nonfarm-business productivity grew <strong>3.6% at a seasonally adjusted annual rate</strong> in the third quarter; output grew <strong>3.7%</strong> and hours worked <strong>0.1%</strong>. This is a quarterly annualized calculation, not a year-over-year increase.</p><a class="source-link" href="https://www.bls.gov/news.release/archives/prod2_11052026.htm" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">BLS · Productivity and Costs, Q3 preliminary · Nov 5</a></div>
          <div class="event" data-claim="payrolls-nov06"><time datetime="2026-11-06">Friday, November 6 · Employment</time><h3>October payroll employment rose by 165,000</h3><p>BLS reported an October increase of <strong>165,000 nonfarm payroll jobs</strong>, with the unemployment rate little changed at <strong>4.1%</strong>. The establishment and household surveys measure different concepts; the issuer-stated release time was <strong>8:30 a.m. Eastern</strong> on November 6.</p><a class="source-link" href="https://www.bls.gov/news.release/archives/empsit_11062026.htm" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">BLS · Employment Situation, October · Nov 6</a></div>
        </div>
      </section>
      <section aria-labelledby="transmission"><div class="section-kicker">03 / Three-dial framework</div><h2 id="transmission">How these facts might reach the dollar</h2>
        <p>There is no automatic one-to-one conversion from an official macro number to a market direction. A transmission mechanism involves expectations about policy, financing conditions and global risk demand, all of which require additional evidence to assess.</p>
        <div class="mechanics">
          <div class="mechanic"><span class="n">01 / LABOR</span><strong>Hiring and mobility</strong><p>JOLTS and payrolls offer different views of labor demand. Changes may influence inflation and policy expectations.</p></div>
          <div class="mechanic"><span class="n">02 / FINANCING</span><strong>Treasury supply and rates</strong><p>Announced security supply sets an auction calendar. Investor demand and auction results determine what actually clears.</p></div>
          <div class="mechanic"><span class="n">03 / EFFICIENCY</span><strong>Productivity and trade</strong><p>Output per hour and external flows add context for growth and costs, not a direct USD pricing signal.</p></div>
        </div>
        <p>In a hypothetical rates-supportive scenario, higher expected U.S. yields could support the dollar, all else equal. In a hypothetical easing scenario, lower expected rates could work differently. Neither scenario establishes actual DXY, EURUSD, gold, WTI, natural gas, Bitcoin or stock-price movements during this week.</p>
      </section>
      <section aria-labelledby="mistakes"><div class="section-kicker">04 / Reader safeguards</div><h2 id="mistakes">Three common mistakes to avoid</h2>
        <p><strong>Publication date is not reference period.</strong> September JOLTS and trade numbers were released in November. October employment and third-quarter productivity use their own measurement windows.</p>
        <p><strong>Announcement is not result.</strong> Treasury announced auction dates for November 9/10/12; all three dates have passed as of this November 13 review, but observed demand, awarded yields and their market implications are <strong>not</strong> in the November 4 statement or this source set.</p>
        <p><strong>Official data is not a price chart.</strong> The five sources cannot determine market consensus surprises, intraday moves, cross-asset returns, causal attribution or an eight-driver USD Impact Score.</p>
      </section>
      <section aria-labelledby="watch"><div class="section-kicker">05 / After-period Treasury chronology</div><h2 id="watch">Treasury auction calendar — as announced November 4</h2>
        <p>The November 4 refunding announcement lists a <strong>$58 billion three-year note auction on Monday, November 9</strong>, a <strong>$42 billion ten-year note auction on Tuesday, November 10</strong>, and a <strong>$25 billion thirty-year bond auction on Thursday, November 12</strong>. The issuer states <strong>1:00 p.m. Eastern</strong> for each. These were announced calendar events as known at the November 6 reporting cutoff, not verified auction outcomes; do not treat a now-past date as upcoming or infer its results.</p>
        <p>Any future addition of auction outcomes first requires separate verification of the November 9, 10 and 12 original Treasury result documents; only with separately rights-cleared, timestamped market data could a later analysis evaluate rates or USD transmission. Other future releases require their own dated issuer evidence.</p>
      </section>
